# 0007. Role fit: a pretrained cross-encoder reranker as the main relevance signal

- Status: Proposed
- Date: 2026-11-17
- Amends: [0002](0002-hybrid-matching.adr) (the responsibilities dimension is replaced)
- Depends on: [0006](0006-eligibility-languages-relocation.adr) (implemented first, so each gain is measured alone)

A pretrained cross-encoder reads your profile and each job together and scores how well the
role fits. It becomes the "role fit" dimension, weighted 50%, replacing the bi-encoder
"responsibilities" dimension. No training: the model is used as published, converted with
PyTorch to ONNX, and served by ONNX Runtime like the embedding model.

## Technical details

**Problem.** On the 250 graded jobs, NDCG@10 is 0.6725 and precision@10 0.60. The main error
is role fit: skill overlap (Python, SQL, AWS) and sentence similarity rank software, analyst
and PM roles as good data-engineering matches. There are 13 positive grades and 16
applications, too few to train a model; the eval grades must never be training data.

**Input pair.**
- Query (yours, built from profile v2, jobradar ADR 0015): `target_roles`, `headline`,
  seniority, the last two titles and the top 10 skills, e.g. "Senior Data Engineer. Wants:
  Data Engineer, Analytics Engineer. Recent: Data Engineer at X. Skills: Spark, Kafka, ...".
  A v1 profile without `target_roles` uses headline and titles.
- Document (the job's): title and company, then the requirement and responsibility sections
  the extractor already finds; the rest of the description only when there are none.
- Long postings are cut into chunks that fit the model (512 tokens), and the best chunk's
  score counts; that chunk is the evidence.

**Scoring.**
- Runs only for jobs whose hard constraints did not fail: excluded jobs are not scored by
  the model (about 90% failed location before ADR 0006; fewer after it).
- Raw logits map to 0..1 by a fixed piecewise-linear calibration from percentiles of the
  model's scores over ~2,000 ungraded jobs, computed at `train` time and stored in the
  bundle (`scoring.yaml`). The eval grades are never used to fit it.
- Weights (`defaults/scoring.yaml`): role_fit 0.50, skills 0.25, seniority 0.10,
  location 0.10, compensation 0.03, employment_type 0.02; `responsibilities` is removed.
- Explanation: "Role fit 82%: 'you will own our Spark and Kafka pipelines...'" (the best
  chunk, cut to 160 characters), plus the existing skill and seniority explanations.

**Model choice: a bake-off.** Candidates, each pinned by repository revision and per-file
sha256 in `data/reranker_model.json`:

| Model | Parameters | Note |
|---|---|---|
| `cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2` | 22M | fastest, English |
| `BAAI/bge-reranker-base` | 278M | stronger, English/Chinese |
| `BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3` | 568M | multilingual (German, French postings) |

Each is converted with PyTorch (`torch.onnx.export`, then int8 dynamic quantization with
`onnxruntime.quantization`) by `matcher export-reranker`, a dev-only command; the bake-off
report (metrics and latency per model) goes in the PR. The best model that meets the bar
below ships; if none does, nothing ships and the report says so.

**Acceptance bar** (eval dataset v1, `matcher eval check`):
- NDCG@10 >= 0.75 and precision@10 >= 0.70 (today 0.6725 and 0.60);
- no new false fails (gates unchanged);
- p95 latency per job <= 2 s on the Mac (today 1.7 s).

**Packaging and determinism.**
- PyTorch is a dev dependency only (`uv` dependency group `export`); the image ships the
  `.onnx` file and `onnxruntime`, as for the embedder: nothing is fetched at runtime, and
  inference is deterministic (fixed threads, no sampling).
- The model files belong to the matcher release (ADR 0004 axis 1), baked into the image; the
  weights and calibration belong to the model version (axis 2) in MLflow.
- Promotion makes the backend re-score all open jobs (jobradar ADR 0010); rollback is
  `matcher promote 5 --force`.

**Baseline:** the acceptance bar is measured against the model version that implements
ADR 0006 (eligibility), not today's v5, so the reranker's own gain is visible.

**Out of scope:** fine-tuning on your applications (a learned layer on top, later), and
translating non-English postings.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: PyTorch and `transformers` (export only), ONNX Runtime (already used),
  `onnxruntime.quantization`, MLflow (ADR 0004).
- Alternatives:
  - Fine-tuning the bi-encoder: needs hundreds of labelled pairs; there are 16.
  - A learned ranker over today's features: same data problem; planned later on top of this.
  - An LLM: excluded (SPEC §13).
  - Serving PyTorch: about 700 MB more image and slower CPU inference than quantized ONNX.
